  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant/dry pattern with comfortably low daytime \nhumidity will last through today before humidity gradually \nclimbs on Sunday. \n\nBroad, low-level streamline convergence with its N/S axis near\nthe RT 219 corridor in Western PA was leading to an expanding\narea of Strato Cu clouds based mainly between 3-4.5 kft agl. A\nfew light showers have also been observed in this area this\nmorning.\n\nAs upper level diffluence slightly increases from the west this\nafternoon and a few embedded flat shortwaves move overhead, \nthere will be isolated to scattered showers with MU CAPE only \n500 J/kg or less. Will leave the isolated afternoon TSRA in for\nnow to the west of RT 219, but would be surprised to see any in\nthat part of our CWA based on the amount of morning cloud cover\nand TDs still only in the 50s to around 60F.\n\nSurface convergence becomes more focused over the eastern half\nof the forecast area for tomorrow. Slightly higher dew points\nwill allow for instability to be a little higher than it is\ntoday, possibly approaching 1000 J/kg in a few locations. Though\nupper level forcing looks to be weak, we would expect to see at\nleast a few showers and thunderstorms develop during the\nafternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Turning more humid and unsettled early next week\nwith the highest probability for showers and thunderstorms \nMonday afternoon and evening and again on Tuesday.\n\nMinor modifications for this period that will be highlighted by\na warm front moving across western PA Monday afternoon/evening,\nfollowed by a rather potent/digging mid/upper level shortwave,\nthat will support a sub-995 mb sfc low over the Lower Glakes and\nWestern NY Tuesday afternoon and evening. Latest model runs have\ntrended several hours or so slower compared to runs 24 hours\nago.  \n\nThe timing of the favorable lift associated with the thermally \nindirect, left front quad of the 80+ KT jet max moving across\nthe Ohio Valley, WVA and southern PA Tuesday afternoon and \nevening will help to enhance a low level lee trough and \nconvection along it. Scattered strong to severe TSRA are likely \nTue afternoon and evening over at least the eastern half of the\nCWA with a second line of TSRA likely along the cold front\nacross NW PA Tuesday afternoon.",
